750,315,17767,282,2075,8,1124,8,525,17767,435,62,16,11,435,62,17,11,435,62,18,11,435,62,19,11,435,62,16,10,20,11,435,62,17,10,20,11,435,62,18,10,20,11,435,62,19,10,20,11,435,62,16,10,21,11,435,62,17,10,21,11,435,62,18,10,21,11,435,62,19,10,21,1124,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30,$ and let $f(x)$ be a polynomial such that\n\\[g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42.\\]Find the sum of all possible values of $f(10^{100}).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\), we start by analyzing the given equation \\( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) where \\( g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30 \\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\( g(f(x)) \\) in terms of \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ g(f(x)) = (f(x))^2 - 11f(x) + 30. \\]\n\nWe know that:\n\\[ (f(x))^2 - 11f(x) + 30 = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \\]\n\nTo find \\( f(x) \\), we need to determine the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\). Let's denote the roots of this polynomial by \\( r_1, r_2, r_3, r_4 \\). Then, we have:\n\\[ (f(x) - r_1)(f(x) - r_2)(f(x) - r_3)(f(x) - r_4) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \\]\n\nSince \\( g(x) = (x-5)(x-6) \\), it follows that \\( f(x) \\) must be one of the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) shifted by 5 or 6. Therefore, the possible values of \\( f(x) \\) are \\( r_1, r_2, r_3, r_4, r_1+5, r_2+5, r_3+5, r_4+5, r_1+6, r_2+6, r_3+6, r_4+6 \\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"e1f15de308c07e1575e62be64dbeb055cb640a47e8eada4fe3175eda0493da80"},"seconds":28.58577275276184,"seed":1090503335320573777,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the roots \\( r_1, r_2, r_3, r_4 \\) of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\), we use the Rational Root Theorem, which suggests that any rational root of the polynomial is a factor of the constant term, 42, divided by a factor of the leading coefficient, 1. The factors of 42 are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 6, \\pm 7, \\pm 14, \\pm 21, \\pm 42 \\).\n\nBy testing these possible roots, we find that \\( x = 1, 2, 3, 7 \\) are the roots of the polynomial. Therefore, the polynomial can be factored as:\n\\[ (x-1)(x-2)(x-3)(x-7). \\]\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are \\( r_1 = 1, r_2 = 2, r_3 = 3, r_4 = 7 \\). Since \\( f(x) \\) can take the values \\( r_i \\) or \\( r_i + 5 \\) or \\( r_i + 6 \\) for \\( i = 1, 2, 3, 4 \\), the possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) are:\n\\[ 1, 2, 3, 7, 6, 7, 8, 12, 7, 8, 9, 13. \\]\n\nSumming these values, we get:\n\\[ 1 + 2 + 3 + 7 + 6 + 7 + 8 + 12 + 7 + 8 + 9 + 13 = 82. \\]\n\nThus, the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{82}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{82}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To determine the roots \\( r_1, r_2, r_3, r_4 \\) of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\), we use the Rational Root Theorem, which suggests that any rational root of the polynomial is a factor of the constant term, 42, divided by a factor of the leading coefficient, 1. The factors of 42 are \\( \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 6, \\pm 7, \\pm 14, \\pm 21, \\pm 42 \\).\n\nBy testing these possible roots, we find that \\( x = 1, 2, 3, 7 \\) are the roots of the polynomial. Therefore, the polynomial can be factored as:\n\\[ (x-1)(x-2)(x-3)(x-7). \\]\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are \\( r_1 = 1, r_2 = 2, r_3 = 3, r_4 = 7 \\). Since \\( f(x) \\) can take the values \\( r_i \\) or \\( r_i + 5 \\) or \\( r_i + 6 \\) for \\( i = 1, 2, 3, 4 \\), the possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) are:\n\\[ 1, 2, 3, 7, 6, 7, 8, 12, 7, 8, 9, 13. \\]\n\nSumming these values, we get:\n\\[ 1 + 2 + 3 + 7 + 6 + 7 + 8 + 12 + 7 + 8 + 9 + 13 = 82. \\]\n\nThus, the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{82}. \\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:58"}
